Yes. You don't need to create a Camunda role assignment for every individual user. The architecture you are describing maps very naturally to OIDC groups → Camunda groups/roles.

For Camunda 8 Self-Managed, the important feature is IdP groups / groups claim. Camunda can read the user's groups from the OIDC token and use those group IDs for authorization.

Your architecture

You could have:

                    ┌──────────────────────┐
                    │   Custom Auth Server  │
                    │   OIDC Provider      │
                    │                      │
                    │ LDAP / AD            │
                    │ users + groups       │
                    └──────────┬───────────┘
                               │
                         OIDC login
                               │
                               ▼
                    ┌──────────────────────┐
                    │    JWT Access Token  │
                    │                      │
                    │ sub: raman           │
                    │ groups:              │
                    │  ["camunda-ops"]     │
                    └──────────┬───────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
                    ┌──────────────────────┐
                    │   Camunda Identity   │
                    │                      │
                    │ group: camunda-ops   │
                    │       ↓              │
                    │ role: Operate        │
                    │ role: Tasklist       │
                    └──────────────────────┘

So imagine your LDAP contains:

Raman
 ├── Engineering
 └── Camunda-Operators

John
 ├── Engineering
 └── Camunda-Users

Alice
 └── Camunda-Admins

Your OIDC server puts that group information into the token:

{
  "sub": "raman",
  "preferred_username": "raman",
  "groups": [
    "Engineering",
    "Camunda-Operators"
  ]
}

Then in Camunda you associate:

Camunda-Operators
        │
        ├── Operate role
        └── Tasklist role

Every user who belongs to Camunda-Operators automatically gets those permissions.

Camunda explicitly supports assigning roles to groups, so members of the group inherit the role's permissions.

The important part: LDAP doesn't necessarily talk directly to Camunda

This is where your custom authentication server comes in.

You can have:

LDAP / Active Directory
        │
        │ user/group information
        ▼
Custom OIDC Provider
        │
        │ JWT containing groups
        ▼
Camunda Identity
        │
        ▼
Tasklist / Operate / Admin

Your custom authentication server is responsible for authenticating:

username + password

against LDAP/whatever user store you have.

Then it issues the OIDC token.

For example:

{
  "iss": "https://auth.company.com",
  "sub": "12345",
  "preferred_username": "raman",
  "email": "raman@company.com",

  "groups": [
    "camunda-task-users",
    "camunda-operators"
  ]
}

Camunda can be configured to read the groups claim. The current Camunda documentation calls this configuration groups-claim; if configured, the group IDs extracted from the token can be used as Camunda group IDs. The IDs need to match exactly.

There are actually two useful approaches
Approach 1 — IdP groups

This is probably what you're describing.

Your auth server sends:

"groups": [
    "camunda-operators"
]

Then Camunda has:

Group: camunda-operators
          │
          ▼
Role: operate-user
          │
          ▼
Operate permissions

This means your application administrators don't have to manage:

Raman → Role
John  → Role
Alice → Role
...

Instead they manage:

LDAP/AD
   │
   └── camunda-operators
          ├── Raman
          ├── John
          └── Alice

And Camunda manages only:

camunda-operators → operate-user

That's a much cleaner model for enterprise environments.

Approach 2 — OIDC mapping rules

Camunda also has mapping rules for OIDC.

For example, suppose your token contains:

{
  "groups": [
    "CAMUNDA_OPERATORS"
  ]
}

You could create a mapping rule such as:

Claim:
groups

Value:
CAMUNDA_OPERATORS

and associate that mapping rule with a role.

Conceptually:

OIDC token
    │
    │ groups = CAMUNDA_OPERATORS
    ▼
Mapping Rule
    │
    ▼
Camunda Role
    │
    ├── Operate
    └── Tasklist

Camunda's mapping rules specifically exist to map JWT claims from the OIDC provider into Camunda access control.

What I would recommend for your architecture

If you control the custom OIDC server, I'd make the group claim a first-class part of the token.

For example:

{
  "sub": "user-123",
  "preferred_username": "raman",
  "groups": [
    "engineering",
    "camunda-task-users"
  ]
}

Then define Camunda access groups around business/application access, not individual people:

LDAP GROUP                         CAMUNDA ACCESS

camunda-task-users       ────────→ Tasklist User
camunda-operators        ────────→ Operate User
camunda-admins           ────────→ Camunda Admin
camunda-modelers         ────────→ Web Modeler

Now onboarding becomes:

New employee
     │
     ▼
LDAP user created
     │
     ▼
Add user to camunda-task-users
     │
     ▼
User logs into Camunda
     │
     ▼
OIDC token contains:
groups = ["camunda-task-users"]
     │
     ▼
Camunda recognizes group
     │
     ▼
Tasklist access

No Camunda UI operation is required for every employee.

One important distinction

There are three different things here:

Authentication
    ↓
"Who are you?"
    ↓
OIDC / Custom Auth Server / LDAP


Group membership
    ↓
"What organization/group do you belong to?"
    ↓
LDAP → OIDC groups claim


Authorization
    ↓
"What can that group do in Camunda?"
    ↓
Camunda Role / Authorization

So you shouldn't try to put all authorization logic into LDAP.

Instead:

LDAP
  ↓
Group membership

OIDC
  ↓
Expose groups in JWT

Camunda
  ↓
Group → Role → Permissions

That separation is clean.

Also, in Camunda 8.7 Self-Managed, Identity is designed to work with users managed by Keycloak or a connected IdP rather than requiring you to manually create/manage every human user in Identity.

One caveat: Camunda 8.7's exact configuration differs from newer 8.8/8.9 documentation, so if you're specifically running 8.7, I can show you the exact application.yaml/environment variables and the exact JWT → LDAP group → Camunda Role flow for 8.7.
